# Backlog: <PROJECT_NAME>

> STARTER TEMPLATE — copy into an application repository as `BACKLOG.md`.
> Replace placeholders and remove setup notes. Example entries are `[-]` so an
> unattended runner cannot treat unfinished templates as approved ready work.

## How to create and maintain this backlog

1. Start from PROJECT.md and the agreed product/architecture decisions. The owner
   and interactive `tech-lead` can draft/revise future work; use `architect` for
   complex slicing.
2. Give each item a permanent unique ID, concrete scope, acceptance criteria, and
   explicit dependencies. Order by priority, with prerequisites ahead of consumers.
3. Keep generated/draft/deferred items `[-]`. Resolve implementation-defining
   questions before promoting work to `[ ]`, or make a separate bounded decision
   task with a verifiable deliverable. Do not queue dependent code on an assumption.
4. The owner approves readiness with `tech-lead`. Runner claims only selected `[ ]`
   items as `[~]`. Never run two claimers against the same file or edit another run's
   active items.
5. Overnight updates outcomes directly for its assigned IDs. `[x]` requires the
   full definition of done. If delivery requires a commit, verify that it succeeded
   before marking done. The agent owns this judgment and the status update.
6. Review blocked/partial work before requeueing. Preserve attempt counts and
   resume locations; do not automatically reset `[!]` or recreate the task under
   a new ID to evade its budget.

Canonical instructions/contracts: `PROJECT.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `<OTHER_REFS_OR_NONE>`.
Execution/commit/review policies live in AGENTS.md; do not duplicate them per item.

## Sprint context — optional

When AGENTS.md selects `sprint-workflow`, name the sprint in this file (for example
`# Sprint v29`) and fill the following metadata. Remove this section for other flows.

- Sprint branch: `<ACTIVE_SPRINT_BRANCH>`
- Version control: `<PROVIDER, E.G. BITBUCKET CLOUD>`

For sprint work, add `Type: feature` or `Type: bug`, `Branch: <name>`, and
`PR: <URL-or-pending>` to each item as applicable. The coordinator manages those fields;
developer may propose a descriptive branch name. The shared skill defines PR targets,
so they need not be repeated here. `[x]` means submitted work is complete; check actual
PR merges separately before dependent work or final sprint verification.

## Status contract

| Marker | Meaning | Owner of transition |
|---|---|---|
| `[ ]` | Approved and ready; prerequisites resolved or explicitly scheduled | Project owner with tech-lead |
| `[~]` | Claimed by a named run; final gates may still be pending | Runner, or explicitly authorized manual coordinator |
| `[x]` | Complete under the project's verification/delivery rules | Assigned coordinator |
| `[!]` | Blocked/incomplete; requires an explicit recovery decision | Assigned coordinator; owner approves requeue |
| `[-]` | Draft, deferred, or intentionally skipped; never automatically selected | Project owner with tech-lead |

Use exactly `## [state] ID — Title` for task headings; ordinary subsections must
not imitate task headings. Existing unique-title backlogs may be handled as a
legacy input, but stable IDs make recovery easier. The supplied helper selects
dependency-eligible ready headings in file order. Declare dependencies as comma-
separated task IDs in a `Depends on:` line; overnight verifies actual integration.
Fenced examples are ignored; duplicate IDs/titles and invalid markers are rejected.
The helper validates positive batch sizes and writes status changes atomically.

`[x]` on a work branch does not mean merged, deployed, or present in another
checkout. The backlog inside the designated project/sandbox checkout is authoritative
for that run. Dependency code must actually exist in its baseline or be verified
earlier in the batch. Reconcile other checkouts deliberately by ID during review.

## Execution metadata

- Run ID and execution branch/worktree: `<SUPPLIED_AT_LAUNCH>`.
- Recovery state: `.opencode/progress.md` (coordinator-owned, normally uncommitted).
- Final report: one summary in DONE.md per batch, matching the existing runner.
- Batch selection: `<ASSIGNED_IDS_OR_RUNNER_POLICY>`.
- A coherent documentation batch: `<RELATED_ITEMS_OR_FEATURE_BOUNDARY>`.
- Retry/deadline policy: `<FINITE_LIMITS_OR_LINK_TO_AGENTS_MD>`.

The runner resets unfinished `[~]` items to `[ ]`; it does not remove partial code
or clear previous progress notes. On resumption, the agent inspects Git history,
the current changes, and those notes before deciding what remains to be done.
It performs verification, commits when authorized, and updates this file directly.
For sprint-workflow, the runner stops after one task. Record the final outcome and
PR URL in a task-branch commit/push so a human merge brings them into the sprint;
fetch and reconcile this file on the sprint branch before a later runner invocation.
The runner accepts uncommitted files; it does not require a cleanup commit before
a run. Preserve partial task-branch work rather than switching away from it to
start a new sprint task.
Tech-lead may publish owner-approved PROJECT.md, AGENTS.md, and future BACKLOG.md
planning changes directly to an existing sprint branch between runs. It does not
rewrite completed outcomes or change a running item's status.

Do not add repetitive status essays. For blocked work, add one short `Blocked:`
line and link the checkpoint. Commit IDs and detailed verification belong in the
checkpoint; avoid inserting a commit's own hash into the file it will commit.

